--TEST--
The four implicit sites are the only ones: an argument declared `string` refuses a `Stringable` object, in a program's own method and in a `Core` member alike — `rule:classes/stringable`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:classes/stringable
final class Money implements Stringable {
    public function constructor(private int $cents) {}

    public function toString(): string {
        return $this->cents . " c";
    }
}

final class Receipt {
    public static function line(string $text): string {
        return "* " . $text;
    }
}

var $m = new Money(250);

// `rule:classes/stringable` names four positions that convert an object implicitly:
// `echo`, `.`, interpolation and `as string`. An argument is not one of them, so
// both calls below are type errors and `Receipt::line($m as string)` is the
// spelling that works.
echo Receipt::line($m), "\n";
echo Core\Str::length($m), "\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `Money`
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
